   221	// P1T-FIX2, review finding N4: a producer deleter that throws a C++ exception must not terminate the process. Every
   222	// deleter call in tc_signal goes through reset() (the immediate release of a refused call, the deferred drain, the
   223	// bound drain, the fallback sync, synchronize() and the atexit hook all end here), and reset() is noexcept, so an
   224	// escaping exception used to reach std::terminate (round-2 probe cpu_native throw_deleter, rc -6). Now each call is
   225	// wrapped: the exception is caught (catch-all), counted (pending_stats()['deleter_exceptions']) and dropped; the owner
   226	// pointer is cleared BEFORE the call, so a deleter is never called twice, and the caller (a loop over an entry's
   227	// managers) keeps going with the remaining managers. The DLPack ABI declares the deleter as a C function, so a
   228	// throwing deleter already breaks the producer's contract; containing it is the consumer's choice, not a recovery
   229	// (the producer's memory may or may not have been freed: tc_signal does not touch it again either way).
   230	// Prior art: C++11 noexcept semantics ([except.terminate]: an exception escaping a noexcept function calls
   231	// std::terminate) and the "destructors/cleanup must not throw" guideline (C++ Core Guidelines C.36/E.16, Sutter &
   232	// Alexandrescu 2004, item 51). Taken: catch at the noexcept boundary. Ours: counting and continuing per manager.
   233	// Known gap (not closed): a deleter that calls pthread_exit or is cancelled unwinds with abi::__forced_unwind, which
   234	// may not be swallowed (glibc aborts); no C++ handler can contain that.
   235	namespace {
   236	std::atomic<unsigned long long> g_deleter_exceptions{0};  // static storage: counting never allocates
   237	
   238	template <typename M>
   239	void call_deleter_contained(M* m) noexcept {
   240	  if (m == nullptr || m->deleter == nullptr) return;
   241	  try {
   242	    m->deleter(m);
   243	  } catch (...) {
   244	    g_deleter_exceptions.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed);
   245	  }
   246	}
   247	}  // namespace
